.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-input, \-I
\fIFILE\fP
.br
Input GMT GRD format bathymetry grid file. This option is required.

.TP
.B \-\-no-binary
Write text-based GLTF (a \fI.gltf\fR file of JSON with the buffers embedded) in place of the binary GLTF (GLB, a \fI.glb\fR file) that is written by default. Binary GLTF is more compact and typically loads faster. \fB\-\-binary\fP, \fB\-B\fP are still accepted and do nothing, since binary is the default.

.TP
.B \-\-output, \-O
\fIROOT\fP
.br
Specify the output file root. The appropriate file extension (.gltf or .glb) will be appended automatically. Default: input file basename in the same directory.

.TP
.B \-\-exaggeration, \-E
\fINUM\fP
.br
Specify the vertical exaggeration factor as a decimal number. The relief is scaled about the mean altitude of the grid, so the model stays in place and each altitude's distance from the mean is multiplied by the factor. This can enhance the visibility of topographic features. The result does not depend on the GeoOrigin. Default: 1.0 (no exaggeration).

.TP
.B \-\-geoorigin[=\fILON,LAT,ELEV\fP], \-G[\fILON,LAT,ELEV\fP]
.br
Specify a GeoOrigin point for high-precision local coordinates. All vertex coordinates will be expressed as offsets from this origin point in the ECEF coordinate system. This improves rendering precision for localized areas by reducing the magnitude of coordinate values, which helps overcome GPU single-precision floating point limitations.
.br
With values (e.g., --geoorigin=-122.0,36.8,-1000): use specified GeoOrigin.
.br
Without values: automatically compute and use grid center and mean altitude.
.br
Not specified: vertices use original ECEF coordinates from Earth's center (default).
.br
The value must be attached to the option. For the long form write an equals sign and no spaces, as in --geoorigin=-122.0,36.8,-1000; for the short form put the value directly after -G, as in -G-122.0,36.8,-1000. A value given as a separate argument, such as --geoorigin -122.0,36.8,-1000, is not accepted, and neither is a value with spaces unless it is quoted so that the shell passes it as one argument.
.br
GLB positions are 32-bit floats, so absolute ECEF (magnitudes of about 6.4 million meters) resolves only about 0.5 m; use a GeoOrigin to preserve sub-meter detail.

.TP
.B \-\-no-draco
Write the mesh as arrays. By default the mesh is compressed with Draco (the glTF extension
\fBKHR_draco_mesh_compression\fP), as mbmesh(1) compresses its meshes, with the code that the two programs
share (\fBsrc/mbdraco\fP). \fB\-\-draco\fP, \fB\-D\fP are still accepted and do nothing, since compression is the default.

.TP
.B \-\-draco-precision \fIMETERS\fP
With Draco, keep the positions to this many meters (default 0.001). Each block of the model gets the number of bits
(8 to 30) that makes the step of its quantization no more than this over its extent, so a small block gets few bits and a large one more.
This replaces \fB\-\-quantize-position\fP and its fixed 16 bits, which gave a step that depended on the size of the block. The options
\fB\-\-quantize-position\fP, \fB\-\-quantize-normal\fP, \fB\-\-quantize-texcoord\fP, \fB\-\-quantize-color\fP and \fB\-Q\fP are refused with a message that names the new options.

.TP
.B \-\-draco-normal-bits \fINUM\fP
With Draco, the bits of each normal, 2 to 30 (default 10). The mesh has no normals today, so this changes nothing; it is the option that mbmesh(1) takes.

.TP
.B \-\-html, \-W
Generate an HTML viewer page with inline 3D visualization using X3DOM. The HTML file includes the glTF/GLB model with interactive navigation controls, processing provenance metadata (command line, timestamps, grid parameters, GeoOrigin settings, compression options), and links to MB-System and X3DOM documentation. The viewer requires serving the files via HTTP to avoid browser CORS restrictions when loading local files. The program does not start a web server or a browser; it logs the command to run (python3 -m http.server 8000 --bind 127.0.0.1 --directory <output directory>) and the URL to open. The processing log is written to <output_root>.log, one message a line, and the page loads it when its Processing Log panel is first opened, so the page need not hold it. Output file: <output_root>.html

.TP
.B \-\-geoviewpoint=\fIVALUES\fR
With \fB\-\-html\fR, add a GeoViewpoint to the page; the option can be repeated, and each
adds a GeoViewpoint, written with its position as latitude, longitude and elevation and the GeoOrigin used again, named USER_GEOVIEW_1, USER_GEOVIEW_2 and so on, to the Viewpoint menu of the
page, after the two overview viewpoints, where Page Down also steps through them. The
values are geodetic, in the order of \fB\-\-geoorigin\fR, longitude first, so they do not depend on the
GeoOrigin or the data and can be used again in another run: \fIlon,lat,elev,clon,clat,celev\fR, the camera
position and the point it looks at, or \fIlon,lat,elev,ax,ay,az,angle,clon,clat,celev\fR, with the
orientation as an axis and an angle, as a GeoViewpoint takes it. Heights are above the
ellipsoid. Without an orientation the top of the view is toward local up. The
\fBCopy GeoViewpoint\fR button of the page gives the ten-number form for the view on the screen, on
the clipboard and in the X3DOM log. The value must be attached to the option, with an
equals sign and no spaces, for example \fB\-\-geoviewpoint=\-122.4,36.6,\-900,\-122.39,36.61,\-950\fR.

.TP
.B \-\-verbose, \-V
Enable verbose output showing NetCDF reading details and memory allocation information.

.TP
.B \-\-help, \-H
Display help message and exit.

.SH GEOREFERENCE AND PROVENANCE METADATA
The glTF file records where the model is on the Earth and how it was made in its
\fBasset.extras\fR, in three blocks. \fBgeoreference\fR gives the coordinate reference
system (\fBEPSG:4978\fR), whether the vertices are \fBabsolute\fR or \fBrelativeToCenter\fR,
the \fBcenter\fR (the GeoOrigin as ECEF meters, with absolute = vertex + center), the same
point as \fBgeodeticCenter\fR (longitude, latitude, height), the \fBheightReference\fR (the
ellipsoid) and the \fBextent\fR of the grid. \fBprovenance\fR gives the software and
version, the time of execution (UTC), the command line, working directory, user and host,
and the input grid file. \fBMB-System\fR gives the grid size, spacing and registration, the
vertical exaggeration, the vertical reference, and \fBcontent.compression\fP (the Draco bits and step) when the mesh is compressed. The page written
by \fB\-\-html\fR holds the same in X3D \fBGeoMetadata\fR and \fBWorldInfo\fR nodes. The
names are shared with mbmesh(1) and are described in \fBsrc/mbmesh/GEOREFERENCE.md\fR.
The grid value is used as a height above the WGS 84 ellipsoid, with no geoid correction.

.SH EXAMPLES
Convert a GMT GRD file to a GLTF file with default settings:
.RS
.nf
.B mbgrd2gltf --input input.grd
.fi
.RE

Convert a GRD file to GLB (Binary GLTF) with specified output folder, keeping the positions to a centimeter (Draco compression is the default):
.RS
.nf
.B mbgrd2gltf --input input.grd --binary --output /path/to/output/mymodel --draco-precision 0.01
.fi
.RE

Convert with vertical exaggeration, without compression, and verbose output:
.RS
.nf
.B mbgrd2gltf --input input.grd --output /path/to/output/mymodel --binary --no-draco --exaggeration 20 --verbose
.fi
.RE

Convert bathymetry with 10x vertical exaggeration:
.RS
.nf
.B mbgrd2gltf --input bathymetry.grd --exaggeration 10.0 --binary
.fi
.RE

Convert bathymetry with a GeoOrigin for improved precision:
.RS
.nf
.B mbgrd2gltf --input monterey.grd --geoorigin=-122.0,36.8,-1000 --binary
.fi
.RE

Convert using automatic GeoOrigin (grid center):
.RS
.nf
.B mbgrd2gltf --input monterey.grd --geoorigin --binary
.fi
.RE

Generate HTML viewer with 3D visualization:
.RS
.nf
.B mbgrd2gltf --input bathymetry.grd --exaggeration 10 --html --binary
.fi
.RE

Legacy syntax (still supported):
.RS
.nf
.B mbgrd2gltf -I input.grd -B -O /path/to/output/mymodel -D -Qp 20 -E 10 -V
.fi
.RE

